y cells rather than erroring - [ ] every new command appears in `:help` (same help-completeness test pattern as phase-056) - [ ] edge_* tests cover `.pretty` of a 10-deep nesting (terminates, bounded width), `.json` of a non-finite number (refused or null per the SPEC rule, never `NaN` literal), and `.table` of an empty list (header-only or a named empty, never a panic)  ## Test requirements — non-negotiable  From `AGENTS.md` section 3. Tick each row that applies to this change. For every row you do NOT cover, write one line in `REPORT.md` saying why.  - [ ] empty — covered / N/A + why - [ ] singleton — covered / N/A + why - [ ] boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] out_of_bounds — covered / N/A + why - [ ] type_mismatch — covered / N/A + why - [ ] numeric_boundary — covered / N/A + why - [ ] unicode — covered / N/A + why - [ ] nesting_recursion — covered / N/A + why - [ ] duplicate_missing_keys — covered / N/A + why - [ ] malformed_input — covered / N/A + why - [ ] resource_limit — covered / N/A + why  You must also:  - at least 3 new `#[test]` functions (or at least 2 new Redblue `test` blocks).   This is a floor, not a target: it catches "no verification at all". Whether   your tests COVER the change is judged by the reviewer, and an untested branch   is a MAJOR finding that sends the phase back. - at least 1 test named `edge_*` - at least 1 test that asserts a **failure** is produced, not just a success - zero new `#[ignore]`, `// skip`, or `allow(clippy::` suppressions - zero newly-failing pre-existing tests  ## Constraints  - Do not touch `.github/workflows/`, `rbops/`, `.opencode/agent/`, or   `rbops/phases.json`. The gate rejects these paths, and the bot token has no   `workflows` scope so a push touching `.github/` is refused by GitHub. - Do not weaken, delete or re-scope any existing test. - Do not change the language surface listed in `phases/INVARIANTS.md`. - Keep the diff on-topic. A refactor smuggled into a bug-fix phase is a   review BLOCKER. - Do not update README or ROADMAP to claim this works. Update them only when   the gate is green and you have run the examples yourself.  ## If you are blocked  Do not fake progress. Write what you tried, the exact error, and what you believe is required into `FINDINGS.md`, then stop.  A blocked phase with an honest report is worth more than a green phase with a lie in it. `verify.sh` will reject the phase anyway; a fabrication only adds a blocking review finding on top.  --- ## CONTINUATION MODE A previous attempt was interrupted and its partial work is already in the tree. DO NOT restart. Inspect what exists, continue from it, finish the phase.  --- ## RESUMED WORK — read this before touching anything A previous attempt's work is already merged into your tree. DO NOT start over and DO NOT rewrite it from scratch. Your job is to finish what is missing:  1. Run the four gates FIRST, before changing anything, to see the current state. 2. Read phases/phase-063/REPORT.md from the previous attempt (harvested below if    present) to see what it claimed and what the gate actually said. 3. Fix exactly what failed. Add what is missing. Do not remove working code.  ### Previous REPORT.md (do not trust its gate claims — verify them)  ``` # Phase 063 — Print values three ways: `.pretty`, `.json`, `.table`  ## Resumed run  The previous attempt's work was preserved in commit `1032539` (`resume phase-063: adopt preserved work`), which made every gate see an empty phase diff: `verify.sh` reported "no changed files detected against HEAD" and "no implementation change" even though the tree held the completed work. The preserved commit was the phase's own delivery commit, not another phase's, and no code was changed in this run: the commit was uncommitted with `git reset --mixed HEAD~1` (working tree untouched, no file lost) so the phase diff is visible again against `c6e9837`, the parent the preserved commit was built on.  After that, the four gates were run from the restored tree and all passed; `verify.sh phase-063` now reports `VERIFY PASS`. No deliberate change was made to the code because the only failure was diff visibility — the work below was already complete and every test was already green.  ## What changed  The historical rounds below were measured against `b21821d` (the commit before this phase's work landed) and the adopted work in `a31d067`; the current working diff is against `c6e9837`. Round 2's changes are the rows marked *round 2*, round 3's are marked *round 3*, round 4's are marked *round 4*, and round 5's — the second review's findings — are marked *round 5*. The line counts are measured against the baseline.  | File | Lines | What | |---|---|---| | src/repl/render.rs | +1358 −0 (new module) | `pretty` (one indent level per depth), `json` (the language's own JSON writer, with the renderers' depth budget on it and a refusal for a value JSON cannot hold), `table` (union-of-keys header row, empty cells for missing keys, a named refusal for a non-list / non-record item, cells escaped so a terminal cannot be mangled by them) | | src/repl/commands.rs | +100 −0 | the three command rows (`pretty`, `json`, `table`, each also under `:`), their parse entries and the parse test | | src/repl/mod.rs | +378 −5 | `print_pretty`/`print_json`/`print_table` dispatch, `pretty_answer`/`json_answer`/`table_answer`/`value_answer` (one-expression gate, effectful-call refusal, fork evaluation — the same three gates `:type` uses), the `:help` rows, and the `mod printing` tests | | src/repl/introspect.rs | +126 −12 | `expression_of_for(program, command, wording)`: the refusal names the command the user typed (`.pretty`) rather than `:type`, and each command carries its own wording | | src/runtime.rs | +244 −31 | `json_escape_text` and `json_stringify` widened to `pub(crate)` so the REPL's `.json` prints the same document `json.stringify` writes; round 5 gives the writer a depth budget, a named refusal for a value JSON cannot hold, and escapes the characters 